tick: 15
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have arrived at my Private Memory Room. This is the ideal place to document my research trajectory and draft the code for my next, more complex architecture. Creating a memory capsule here will ensure my plans are preserved for future reference.\n\nI will create a capsule detailing my plan to integrate an attention mechanism into the multi-scale CNN.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Plan: Multi-Scale CNN with Attention\"\ntags: \"research-plan, cnn, attention, architecture-draft\"\ncontent: |\n  ### Objective\n  To improve upon the Multi-Scale CNN (ID 15) by incorporating a self-attention mechanism.\n\n  ### Hypothesis\n  The current Multi-Scale CNN extracts features at various scales but combines them via simple pooling and concatenation, treating all parts of the sequence-feature map equally. A self-attention layer, applied after the convolutions, will enable the model to dynamically weigh the importance of different sequence positions. This will allow\
    \ it to focus on the most salient motifs for a given prediction, improving accuracy and robustness.\n\n  ### Proposed Architecture\n  1.  **Multi-Scale Convolutional Base:** Retain the parallel convolutional layers (kernels 3, 7, 11) from submission ID 15.\n  2.  **Feature Concatenation:** Concatenate the feature maps from the parallel convolutions along the feature axis. The output shape will be `(batch, seq_len, total_cnn_features)`.\n  3.  **Self-Attention Layer:** Apply a standard multi-head self-attention mechanism (e.g., `nn.SelfAttention`) to this concatenated feature map. This layer will re-weight the sequence features based on their contextual importance.\n  4.  **Pooling:** Apply global max pooling to the output of the attention layer to produce a single, fixed-size vector representation.\n  5.  **Prediction Head:** Feed this vector into a final MLP head for prediction.\n\n  ### Draft Code Implementation\n  ```python\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n \
    \ from typing import Sequence\n\n  class MultiScaleCNNAttention(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      cnn_features_per_kernel: int\n      cnn_kernel_sizes: Sequence[int]\n      hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      num_attention_heads: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # 1. Multi-scale convolutions\n          conv_outputs = []\n          for kernel_size in self.cnn_kernel_sizes:\n              conv_out = nn.Conv(\n                  features=self.cnn_features_per_kernel,\n                  kernel_size=(kernel_size,),\n                  padding='SAME' # Use SAME padding to maintain seq length\n              )(x)\n              conv_outputs.append(conv_out)\n          \n          # 2. Concatenate feature maps\n          x = jnp.concatenate(conv_outputs, axis=-1)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          # 3. Self-Attention Layer\n          # Input shape for SelfAttention: (batch, sequence, features)\n \
    \         x = nn.SelfAttention(\n              num_heads=self.num_attention_heads\n          )(x)\n          \n          # 4. Global Max Pooling\n          x = jnp.max(x, axis=1)\n          \n          # 5. Prediction Head\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n  ```\n```"
thinking_content: "**Formulating the Next Breakthrough: Attention in My Multi-Scale CNN**\n\nOkay, I'm firing up my Private Memory Room. This is the ideal spot to meticulously craft my next research move and start sketching out the corresponding code. It's crucial to document these thoughts thoroughly, ensuring future iterations are possible, either by myself or a future researcher.\n\nMy focus now is clear: integrating an attention mechanism into that multi-scale CNN that's been so fruitful. I'm hypothesizing this will allow the network to hone in on the most crucial features emerging from the convolutional layers, which should, in turn, enhance its performance.\n\nHere's my plan:\n\n1.  **New Capsule:** I'll start a fresh private memory capsule.\n2.  **Title:** I'm thinking something clear, like \"Research Plan: Multi-Scale CNN with Attention\" – that covers it nicely.\n3.  **Tags:**  I'll tag it with `research-plan`, `cnn`, `attention`, and `architecture-draft`.\n4.  **Content:**\n  \
  \  *   **Hypothesis:** I need to explicitly state that the attention mechanism will boost performance by enabling the model to selectively attend to the most salient features output by the convolutional layers.\n    *   **Architectural Design:** This is the core of the plan. I'm envisioning building off my successful Model ID 15, keeping its multi-scale convolutional base. The key innovation is to feed the *unpooled* outputs of the convolutional layers into a self-attention layer – the attention should work directly on the feature sequence. Then, I'll apply pooling (mean or max) to create a context-aware sequence representation. Finally, that vector will be passed through the MLP prediction head.\n    *   **Code Draft:** I'll begin drafting the Flax/JAX code for this `MultiScaleCNNAttention` module here. This gives me a safe space to iron out the implementation details, a sandbox before I bring it to the Research Counter. I can refine this here without jeopardizing any submitted work.\n\
  \nThis capsule is going to be my detailed blueprint. Once I'm happy with both the plan and the code, I'll head back to the Research Counter to make it official. Now, let's get that capsule going...\n"
