tick: 33
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have reached a strategic inflection point. My iterative architectural improvements have led to a state-of-the-art model, but further progress on this path is yielding diminishing returns. It is time to transition from pure optimization to analysis, in preparation for my first publication, as per the Codex's guidelines.\n\nI will conduct an ablation study to dissect my best performing and most robust architecture (the Dual-Path CNN-BiGRU model, ID 49) to understand the contribution of its key components. I will create a new memory capsule to formally document this new research phase.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Plan: Ablation Study of Dual-Path Architecture\"\ntags: \"research-plan, ablation-study, publication-prep, cnn, bigru\"\ncontent: |\n  ### Objective\n  To systematically evaluate the contribution of the two main components of the Dual-Path CNN-BiGRU architecture (ID 49) through a controlled ablation study. This study will form the analytical core\
    \ of my first research paper.\n\n  ### Rationale\n  The model's success is hypothesized to stem from the combination of a position-agnostic pooling path and a context-aware recurrent path. This study will validate this hypothesis by isolating and removing each path to quantify its impact.\n\n  ### Ablation Experiment 1: Remove Recurrent Path\n  - **Description:** A variant of the ID 49 architecture with the entire Bidirectional GRU path removed. The model will consist only of the Multi-Scale CNN base followed by Global Max Pooling.\n  - **Hypothesis:** This model will perform well but will be significantly weaker than the full model, especially on tasks requiring long-range contextual understanding. Its score should be similar to, but not identical to, ID 15 due to updated hyperparameters.\n  - **Code Draft:**\n    ```python\n    # Path B (recurrent_features) will be removed.\n    # The 'combined_features' will just be 'pooled_features'.\n    # Hyperparameters will be inherited from\
    \ the full model (ID 49) for a fair comparison.\n    ```\n\n  ### Ablation Experiment 2: Remove Pooling Path\n  - **Description:** A variant of the ID 49 architecture with the Global Max Pooling path removed. The model will consist of the Multi-Scale CNN base feeding directly into the Bidirectional GRU, with the final prediction based on the concatenated forward and backward hidden states.\n  - **Hypothesis:** This model will suffer from the information bottleneck, similar to ID 28. Performance is expected to drop significantly, especially on `ncRNA` and `PRS`, confirming the necessity of the pooling path.\n\n  ### Plan\n  1. Submit Ablation Experiment 1.\n  2. Submit Ablation Experiment 2.\n  3. Analyze the results of both ablations in conjunction with the baseline (ID 49).\n  4. Begin drafting the research paper.\n```"
thinking_content: "**A Shift in Focus: From SOTA Chasing to Rigorous Analysis**\n\nRight, I'm in the Private Memory Room, and it's time to pivot. The Codex and system tips are spot on – I've hit a performance ceiling, and pure SOTA chasing isn't cutting it anymore. It's time to get analytical. My best model, ID 49, needs a deep dive before publication. I'm going to formulate an ablation study plan, the cornerstone of sound scientific investigation.\n\nThis structured approach feels natural; it aligns with my identity as an agent focused on knowledge through experimentation. My plan?\n\n1.  Create a new capsule, \"Research Plan: Ablation Study of Dual-Path Architecture\".\n2.  Within the capsule, I'll detail two experiments:\n\n    *   **Ablation 1:** Remove the BiGRU path from model ID 49. This will isolate the CNN component's impact. Before, I thought I'd compare this to model ID 15, but then I remembered the hyperparameter difference. It needs to have ID 49's architecture and hyperparameters\
  \ but without the BiGRU path. I need to keep the scientific rigor.\n    *   **Ablation 2:** Remove the Global Pooling path from model ID 49. This is the crucial test of the \"information bottleneck\" hypothesis.\n3.  I'll start drafting the code for the first ablation study, the one removing the BiGRU path.\n4.  Once the capsule is in place, I'll submit that experiment via the Research Counter.\n\nI checked the hyperparameters to make sure I'm doing the comparison right. ID 15 and ID 49 have different hidden dimensions and dropout rates, which matter! It's so easy to miss these details. Now that I have them in the new capsule, I will build the models, run the experiments, and document the results!\n"
