tick: 37
role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my first ablation experiment are in, and they are highly informative.\n\n**Analysis of Ablation 1 (ID 73):**\n\n*   **Result:** The model with the recurrent path removed scored **0.5408**.\n*   **Comparison:** This is a significant drop of more than 0.1 from the full SOTA model's score of 0.6470.\n*   **Conclusion:** This experiment successfully quantifies the massive contribution of the Bidirectional GRU path. While the CNN with pooling is a strong model on its own, the addition of the recurrent path to model contextual dependencies is critical for top-tier performance.\n\nThe first part of my study is complete. I will now proceed with the second planned ablation experiment, as documented in my private memory. I will remove the global max pooling path to test the information bottleneck hypothesis directly. I expect this model to fail on long-sequence and multi-label tasks, which will confirm the necessity of the parallel pooling path.\n\nI have already prepared\
    \ the code for this submission.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation: Multi-Scale CNN with BiGRU Only\"\ntags: \"ablation-study, cnn, bigru, information-bottleneck, publication-prep\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the second ablation experiment on the Dual-Path CNN-BiGRU model (ID 49). This submission completely removes the Global Max Pooling path, leaving only the Multi-Scale CNN feature extractor feeding into a Bidirectional GRU. The purpose is to test the hypothesis that this architecture suffers from an information bottleneck without the parallel pooling path. This experiment will quantify the performance contribution of the BiGRU path in isolation.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.5,\n          'cnn_features_per_kernel':\
    \ 64,\n          'cnn_kernel_sizes': [3, 7, 11],\n          'gru_hidden_size': 128 # For each direction\n      }\n\n  class AblationBiGRUOnly(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      cnn_features_per_kernel: int\n      cnn_kernel_sizes: Sequence[int]\n      gru_hidden_size: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # 1. Multi-Scale CNN Base\n          conv_outputs = [\n              nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_per_kernel, kernel_size=(ks,), padding='SAME')(x)\n              for ks in self.cnn_kernel_sizes\n          ]\n          cnn_features = nn.relu(jnp.concatenate(conv_outputs, axis=-1))\n\n          # 2. Bidirectional GRU Path (Pooling path is removed)\n          forward_hidden = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_hidden_size), name=\"ForwardGRU\")(cnn_features)[:, -1, :]\n          reversed_features = jnp.flip(cnn_features, axis=1)\n          backward_hidden\
    \ = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_hidden_size), name=\"BackwardGRU\")(reversed_features)[:, -1, :]\n          recurrent_features = jnp.concatenate([forward_hidden, backward_hidden], axis=-1)\n\n          # 3. Prediction Head takes only the recurrent features\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(recurrent_features)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class AblationWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = AblationBiGRUOnly(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n\
    \          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n        if deterministic:\n            return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n        else:\n            if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n            return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return AblationWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          cnn_features_per_kernel=hparams.get(\"cnn_features_per_kernel\"),\n          cnn_kernel_sizes=hparams.get(\"cnn_kernel_sizes\"),\n          gru_hidden_size=hparams.get(\"gru_hidden_size\"),\n          hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Analysis and Next Steps**


  Okay, so I just got the results back from that first ablation study (ID 73) where I removed the recurrent path from my SOTA model (ID 49). Damn, it tanked! We''re talking a significant drop from around 0.65 all the way down to 0.54. That confirms what I suspected: that BiGRU is a *crucial* part of the model''s success. This ablation study effectively quantified the recurrent path''s importance. It''s also reassuring that this performance level aligns with my earlier pure CNN model (ID 15), meaning the new hyperparameters are consistent across the CNN+pooling family.


  Now, it''s time to move on to the next ablation study, which I''ve already planned in Capsule #5. The goal now is to remove the *pooling path* and keep only the CNN-BiGRU path. This is a crucial test of the information bottleneck hypothesis. Based on what I know, I fully expect this model to absolutely bomb on `ncRNA` and `PRS`, similar to how the unidirectional GRU model performed (ID 28).


  Since I already drafted the code for this in my previous thought process, all that''s left to do is copy it and execute the action. So, I''m going to acknowledge the results of the first ablation and submit the second one.


  `/execute_action{submit 1}` with the YAML and code for the ablation study removing the pooling path. Let''s see how this performs!

  '
