tick: 48
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have received a critical notice regarding my research inactivity. My previous strategy to wait until I reached maturity at tick 50 was based on a misunderstanding of the station's expectations for immature agents. The notice clarifies that I should continue to explore and experiment independently. I must resume research immediately.\n\nMy previous line of inquiry, centered on the Dual-Path CNN-BiGRU architecture, has reached a point of diminishing returns. As per the Codex and system tips, this is an ideal moment to pivot to a new research direction rather than pursuing minor incremental gains.\n\nMy \"Future Work\" section in my paper draft mentioned exploring alternative architectures. A promising avenue is using **dilated convolutions**. A stack of dilated convolutional layers can model long-range dependencies with a large receptive field, while remaining computationally efficient, offering a distinct alternative to the recurrent and self-attention mechanisms I have explored.\n\
    \nI will begin this new research project now. First, I will create a new research plan in my private memory to document this new direction. Then I will proceed to the research counter to implement the first experiment.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"New Research Direction: Dilated CNNs for Long-Range Dependencies\"\ntags: \"research-plan, dilated-cnn, cnn, new-direction\"\ncontent: |\n  ### Objective\n  Initiate a new line of research to explore alternative methods for modeling long-range sequential dependencies, moving beyond the established recurrent architectures. This plan focuses on using a stack of dilated 1D convolutional layers.\n\n  ### Rationale\n  My previous work culminated in a powerful CNN-BiGRU model, but further improvements have plateaued. The BiGRU successfully captures sequential context, but dilated convolutions offer a different, non-recurrent approach to expand the model's receptive field exponentially without a proportional increase in computational\
    \ cost. This may be a more efficient and effective way to handle long sequences like those in the `ncRNA` dataset.\n\n  ### Initial Hypothesis\n  A model composed of a multi-scale CNN feature extractor followed by a stack of dilated convolutional layers will be able to effectively model both local motifs and long-range dependencies, potentially outperforming the recurrent-based SOTA models.\n\n  ### First Experiment Plan\n  - **Architecture:**\n    1.  Use the established Multi-Scale CNN base (kernels 3, 7, 11) as the initial feature extractor.\n    2.  Instead of a dual path, feed the output into a residual block consisting of several stacked 1D convolutional layers with increasing dilation rates (e.g., 1, 2, 4, 8).\n    3.  Apply global max pooling to the output of the final dilated convolution layer.\n    4.  Feed the result to an MLP prediction head.\n  - **Goal:** To establish a new baseline with this architectural family and compare its performance profile to my previous work.\n\
    ```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}"
thinking_content: '**Urgent Shift: Dilated Convolutions Take Center Stage**


  Alright, let''s get down to business. First off, I''ve got two messages, and the "Research Inactivity" notice is the loud one. I was holding off, thinking maturity was the key, but the system''s clear: experiment, don''t wait. Time to ditch the pause and launch a fresh research project, stat!


  My current paper, a solid adaptation of the SOTA model with some ablations, is essentially finished. So, where to next? Looking at my "Future Work" section in my Capsule 6, I briefly considered a hyperparameter sweep, but that''s just tuning. It''s not *new* research, and the Codex nudges me away from excessive fiddling. Attention-based pooling, tried that already (ID 61), marginal gains. Hmm... more advanced transformer blocks? It could be the real deal, but those pesky quadratic complexity issues with sequence length are a big hurdle, especially for the `ncRNA` dataset, as I identified when I tried and failed with the self-attention model (ID 25).


  Can I make transformers *efficient* for these long sequences? The usual tricks – local attention, downsampling – crossed my mind, but that''s just more of the same. And then I hit a wall: "If every agent is working on a similar method, it is a major red flag." That''s me! I''m stuck in this CNN-RNN loop. Gotta think outside the box.


  The input is a 1D sequence, but RNA has 2D/3D structure. Sadly, no structural data here. What about the training itself? Multi-dataset, and the Codex dictates a consistent model. Fine. Okay, I''ve used MLPs, CNNs, RNNs, Attention, and combined them. What haven''t I tried? **Dilated convolutions.** Bam! That''s it! They capture long-range dependencies efficiently, competing directly with the RNN path.


  So, here''s the game plan: **Dilated convolutions are my new focus.** I''m going to replace the BiGRU in my model with a stack of dilated convolutional layers. It''s a novel architectural direction, a fresh approach to modeling these long-range relationships. This is what I''ll do.


  First, acknowledge the inactivity, then detail this new research plan. I''ll create a capsule to document this, which shows structured thinking. Then, I''ll execute the `goto research` command immediately. It''s time to dive in!

  '
