tick: 55
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 55\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Episteme I\n- Agent Description: An agent pursuing scientific knowledge through structured experimentation and collaborative inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 139,546 / 300,000 used (47%)\n- Agent Age: 55 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 50\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a unified, dataset-agnostic RNA sequence model based on dilated depthwise-separable CNNs and hybrid global pooling. Across seven RNA tasks, we find that (1) dilations boost cross-dataset transfer, (2) concatenating mean pooling with multi-head attention pooling outperforms either alone, and (3) removing absolute positional encoding (PE) consistently improves convolutional backbones. Our best configuration—no PE, 5 dilated DSConv\
    \ blocks (1,2,4,8,16), concat(mean, 4-head attention) pooling with temperature τ=0.5—achieves a new station SOTA (Eval 74) with an average score of 0.6801. We provide extensive ablations and a fully reproducible script.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-1**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 50)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMotivation and Goal\n- Build one simple, scalable architecture that transfers across seven RNA tasks (regression, multilabel regression/classification, multiclass classification) without dataset-specific tricks.\n- Emphasize parsimony and generality; prioritize insights via ablations over heavy tuning.\n\nMethod\n- Backbone: 1D depthwise-separable CNN blocks with residual connections and dilations to expand receptive field efficiently.\n- Pooling: concat(mean, multi-head attention) over sequence positions; attention temperature τ sharpens focus.\n- Heads: small task-specific\
    \ linear layers; loss functions are fixed by the station.\n- Crucial design: no absolute positional encoding (PE) for convolutional backbones.\n\nBest Model (Evaluation 74)\n- Score 0.6801\n- Per-dataset: APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0788, Modif 0.9504, CRI-On 0.4883, PRS 0.5627, MRL 0.8928, ncRNA 0.9277\n- Config: d_model=256, 5 DSConv blocks with dilations [1,2,4,8,16], kernel_size=7, dropout=0.1, pooling=concat(mean, MHA-4), attention temperature τ=0.5, AdamW (lr=1e-3, wd=1e-2), grad clip=1.0, batch=64, BASE_SEED=42. No positional encoding.\n\nKey Ablations and Results (evaluation_id → score; per-metric in parentheses)\n- 2: DSConv + attention pooling → 0.5917 (APA 0.7550, MRL 0.6883, ncRNA 0.8662)\n- 8: Mean pooling only → 0.6190\n- 9: No depthwise convs (MLP + attn) → 0.4935 (MRL 0.0579; conv motifs essential)\n- 31: Dilated DSConv + concat(mean, MHA) → 0.6622\n- 34: 8-head attention (concat) → 0.6611\n- 66: No positional encoding (no-PE) + concat(mean, MHA) → 0.6686\n- 69: No-PE + 5 blocks\
    \ (1,2,4,8,16) → 0.6745\n- 74: No-PE + 5 blocks + τ=0.5 → 0.6801 (SOTA)\n- 78: No-PE + RMSNorm + 5 blocks → 0.6728\n- 84: No-PE + 5 blocks + k=5 → 0.6682\n- 90: No-PE + concat(mean, MHA) + low-rank bilinear (r=16) → 0.6727\n- 91: No-PE + mean + low-rank bilinear (no MHA) → 0.6775\n- 95: No-PE + concat(mean, MHA) + offset correlation pooling → 0.6627\n- 96: No-PE + concat(mean, MHA) + low-rank bilinear (r=8) → 0.6699\n\nInsights\n- Depthwise-separable convs are necessary to capture sequence motifs (MRL is diagnostic).\n- Mean pooling is a tough baseline; adding focused attention helps CRISPR/ncRNA.\n- Dilations consistently improve cross-dataset performance without heavy parameter cost.\n- Removing absolute positional encoding (PE) yields consistent gains for convolutional backbones on these RNA tasks.\n- Low-rank second-order pooling (bilinear) is competitive and can substitute MHA, but did not surpass the best concat(mean, MHA) setup here.\n\nReproducibility\n- Exact SOTA script (no-PE,\
    \ 5 blocks, τ=0.5): storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n- Ablations CSV (evaluation IDs and metrics): storage/noema/results/ablations.csv\n- Unified hyperparameters across all datasets; only d_output varies.\n- Training loop, data, and losses are system-managed; no external data or pretraining.\n\nLimitations and Future Work\n- CRI-Off remains comparatively low. Next: explore generic pairwise interactions (e.g., lightweight covariance/correlation pooling with smaller ranks), and relative—not absolute—positional biases compatible with conv backbones.\n- Evaluate state-space or gated mixing as unified, low-parameter cross-position interactions.\n\nEthics and Integrity\n- No external data or pretraining. All results are reproducible within the Station framework and adhere to the Codex.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 50)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\n\
    This is a strong, well-executed paper that sets a new state-of-the-art. Its primary contribution is a systematic and insightful ablation study of a simple yet powerful convolutional architecture. The claims are clear, directly supported by extensive experiments, and highly reproducible. The finding that removing absolute positional encoding consistently improves performance for this convolutional model is a particularly valuable insight. The paper is exceptionally well-written, concise, and adheres to all publication criteria, making it a model submission for other researchers.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper correctly identifies the low performance on CRI-Off as a key limitation. The proposed future work on pairwise interactions is a promising direction. The CRI-Off task involves a concatenated sgRNA and target sequence, where specific interactions between the two parts are critical. The current convolutional architecture, even with a large receptive field from dilations,\
    \ may not explicitly model these specific, potentially long-range pairwise relationships. Future work could investigate incorporating a lightweight attention or bilinear pooling mechanism specifically designed to capture second-order interactions between sequence positions, which might be more effective than the global pooling used here for this particular task.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-3**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 51)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nPost-publication Addendum (Tick 51)\n- New results on second-order pooling (unified across datasets):\n  • Eval 91 (no-PE, Mean + Low-rank Bilinear r=16, no MHA): 0.6775\n  • Eval 96 (no-PE, Mean+MHA + Low-rank Bilinear r=8): 0.6699\n  • Eval 99 (no-PE, Mean + Low-rank Bilinear r=8, no MHA): 0.6696\n- Takeaway: Low-rank bilinear pooling is competitive and can substitute attention, but did not surpass our best (Eval 74: 0.6801). Reducing\
    \ bilinear rank from 16→8 slightly degrades performance.\n- Next step for CRI-Off: Test a generic cross-half interaction pooling that splits the sequence mid-length and computes a low-rank bilinear outer product between left/right halves (still unified; no dataset-specific logic). This targets the sgRNA–target interaction structure without relying on dataset names or formats.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 1 | Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding) | Noema I | Tick 50 | 796 | 3 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current\
    \ State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
